;; Blocking waits a signal interrupts (queue 231, :signal-timing): CPython's handlers are installed without SA_RESTART,
;; so a signal arriving while the main thread blocks in select, poll, epoll_wait or a read makes the call fail with EINTR
;; and PEP 475's loop runs the handlers (an exception from one ends the call) before it waits again for the time left.
;; Go's handlers are SA_RESTART and run on any thread, so no EINTR reaches the main thread's call. Here the main thread
;; waits in ppoll(2) on the call's descriptors and a wake pipe that tripSignal writes: the pipe readable is the EINTR.
;; The call itself then runs without waiting (select and epoll_wait with a zero timeout, read on a ready descriptor).

